cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dual-nonlinear-glu: revisiting the linear value branch of gated layers"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - alias: PureTearsDropped
repository-code: "https://github.com/PureTearsDropped/dual-nonlinear-glu"
license: 0BSD
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22851806
abstract: >-
  Gated layers in transformers keep their value branch linear: SwiGLU is
  silu(W_g h) * (W_u h). Making both branches nonlinear is not a new design --
  it is the Gated Tanh Unit, which Dauphin et al. (2017) measured and rejected
  because both branches contribute a gradient downscaling factor, and the
  GLU -> GEGLU -> SwiGLU line descends from that rejection. On a synthetic
  teacher-student benchmark with a residual and a 1/sqrt(depth) branch
  initialisation, that rejection does not reproduce. Two teachers, however,
  give opposite accounts of why: on a product teacher the effect is dual
  nonlinearity itself (4.5x, and silu*silu ties the proposed pair), while on a
  non-product teacher dual nonlinearity buys nothing and only the particular
  pair helps (33%). The block's output is the mixed second derivative of an
  elementary potential F(u)G(v), which is not the case for SwiGLU. Also
  recorded: initialising the residual branch at 1/sqrt(depth) outweighs every
  activation choice measured; curvature-aware ternary rounding was derived,
  corrected, and failed; and a benchmark checklist for detecting tasks that do
  not need depth.
keywords:
  - activation function
  - gated linear unit
  - SwiGLU
  - residual network
  - benchmark methodology
  - negative results
  - gated tanh unit
references:
  - type: software
    title: "relu-tanh-asinh: a conjugate f/g residual layer"
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22847921
